{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Bounded Interval.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Bounded Interval.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"bounded interval bounded interval","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Bounded Interval.md","fileName":"Bounded Interval.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Bounded Interval","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-05 23:41\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Linear Inequalities and Intervals]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Linear Inequalities and Intervals","Real Number","algebraessentials.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Bounded Interval.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Bounded Interval.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"bounded interval bounded interval","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Bounded Interval.md","fileName":"Bounded Interval.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Bounded Interval","heading":"Bounded Interval","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"A bounded interval lies between two finite endpoints. Examples include\n\n$$-1\\leq x\\leq2$$\n\nand\n\n$$-1<x<2.$$\n\nIts length is the upper endpoint minus the lower endpoint, regardless of whether either boundary is included. Although the length is finite, the interval contains infinitely many [[Real Number]]s. Endpoint symbols determine membership: brackets or filled points include a boundary, while parentheses or open points exclude it.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Linear Inequalities and Intervals","Real Number","algebraessentials.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Bounded Interval.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Bounded Interval.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"bounded interval bounded interval","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Bounded Interval.md","fileName":"Bounded Inter